{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Group.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Abel","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Group"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.addSeparable_of_traceFactorisation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.addSeparable_of_traceFactorisation","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/TwoBodyInterfaceBond.lean#L145-L157","doc":"**Factorised traces ⇒ additively separable binding.** If the free trace is\n`tL⁰ a · tR⁰ b` and the atom trace is `tL a · tR b` (all positive), then the live law\n`E₀ · log(tr_free / tr_atom)` is a left term plus a right term. The factorisation is the\nhypothesis; this is the arithmetic that turns it into the arity class. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.twoBody_strict_max_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.twoBody_strict_max_witness","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/TwoBodyInterfaceBond.lean#L135-L141","doc":"**The exclusion is exactly the class.** An exchange-symmetric functional that puts the\nunlike pair strictly above both like pairs exists (so it is not additively separable). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.exchangeSymmetric_not_strict_min","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.exchangeSymmetric_not_strict_min","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/TwoBodyInterfaceBond.lean#L127-L133","doc":"**Nor strictly the least bound.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.exchangeSymmetric_not_strict_max","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.exchangeSymmetric_not_strict_max","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/TwoBodyInterfaceBond.lean#L118-L125","doc":"**So an exchange-symmetric additively separable bond can never make the unlike pair\nstrictly the most bound.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.exchangeSymmetric_midpoint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.exchangeSymmetric_midpoint","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/TwoBodyInterfaceBond.lean#L110-L116","doc":"**Exchange symmetry makes the unlike pair the midpoint.** If moreover `f p n = f n p`,\nthen `f p n + f p n = f p p + f n n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.strict_max_forces_reverse_strict_min","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.strict_max_forces_reverse_strict_min","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/TwoBodyInterfaceBond.lean#L92-L107","doc":"**A strict maximum in one orientation is a strict minimum in the other.** If the unlike\npair `pn` is strictly above both like pairs, the reversed unlike pair `np` is strictly below\nboth. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.cross_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.cross_sum","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/TwoBodyInterfaceBond.lean#L82-L88","doc":"**Cross-sum identity.** For an additively separable functional,\n`f p n + f n p = f p p + f n n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.AddSeparable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.AddSeparable","kind":"def","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/TwoBodyInterfaceBond.lean#L77-L80","doc":"A pair functional is **additively separable** if it is a sum of a left and a right\none-body term. One-body functionals are the special case with one term zero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.vertex21_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.vertex21_eq","kind":"theorem","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/TwoBodyInterfaceBond.lean#L63-L71","doc":"**The other order is the interface-letter conjugate of the same split bond.**\n`(s₂ s₃⁻¹)(s₁ s₂⁻¹) = s₂ (s₃⁻¹ s₁) s₂⁻¹`; with `s₁, s₃` commuting (far-apart braid generators)\nthe bracket is the split bond `s₁ s₃⁻¹` of `vertex12_cancels`. So `v21` and `v12` are conjugate\nelements and have the same character in every block — which is why reading 3 *bond-alone*\ncannot tell them apart (the probe's S1 tie) — while inside the atom the conjugating `s₂` does\nnot commute with the nucleon words, and the probe measures `v21` to be genuinely two-body. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.vertex12_cancels","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.TwoBodyInterfaceBond.vertex12_cancels","kind":"theorem","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/TwoBodyInterfaceBond.lean#L55-L61","doc":"**`vertex 1 · vertex 2` loses its interface letter.** In any group,\n`(s₁ s₂⁻¹)(s₂ s₃⁻¹) = s₁ s₃⁻¹`: the interface generator `s₂` occurs once with each sign,\nadjacently, and cancels. Read with `sᵢ = σᵢ` this is the γγ word `v12` of the probe, which\nis therefore the disjoint-union bond `σ₁σ₃⁻¹` and leaves the two nucleons unlinked. ","signatureChunk":0}]}